WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.890
A controller is useful when a system must
reach and maintain a target despite

2
00:00:05.890 --> 00:00:07.142
disturbances and uncertainty.

3
00:00:07.142 --> 00:00:11.977
Imagine a motor commanded to run at one
thousand revolutions per minute.

4
00:00:11.977 --> 00:00:17.244
An open-loop command may work under one load
and miss the target under another.

5
00:00:17.244 --> 00:00:19.897
Feedback changes the structure of the problem.

6
00:00:19.897 --> 00:00:25.465
A sensor measures actual speed, the controller
compares it with the desired speed, and the

7
00:00:25.465 --> 00:00:27.321
difference becomes an error signal.

8
00:00:27.321 --> 00:00:30.803
The controller uses that error to change the
motor input.

9
00:00:30.803 --> 00:00:37.040
The new speed is measured again, so correction
is continuous rather than a one-time guess.

10
00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:42.608
This lesson develops the proportional,
integral, and derivative terms, then connects

11
00:00:42.608 --> 00:00:48.176
their behavior to tuning, noise, saturation,
and a practical design workflow.

12
00:00:48.314 --> 00:00:50.725
Start by naming every signal.

13
00:00:50.725 --> 00:00:53.621
The reference r of t is the desired behavior.

14
00:00:53.621 --> 00:00:57.230
The measured output y of t is what the sensor
reports.

15
00:00:57.230 --> 00:01:02.332
Their difference, e of t, equals reference
minus measured output.

16
00:01:02.332 --> 00:01:05.623
The controller converts error into a command u
of t.

17
00:01:05.623 --> 00:01:11.408
An actuator applies that command to the plant,
which is the physical or simulated system

18
00:01:11.408 --> 00:01:12.179
being controlled.

19
00:01:12.179 --> 00:01:17.830
Disturbances can enter the plant, while sensor
noise can enter the measurement path.

20
00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:23.397
This vocabulary prevents a common mistake: the
controller does not directly control the

21
00:01:23.397 --> 00:01:23.825
error.

22
00:01:23.825 --> 00:01:29.226
It changes the plant input, observes the
resulting output, and thereby influences

23
00:01:29.226 --> 00:01:30.126
future error.

24
00:01:30.126 --> 00:01:32.613
Sign conventions also matter.

25
00:01:32.613 --> 00:01:37.656
With negative feedback, a positive error
should normally produce a correction that

26
00:01:37.656 --> 00:01:40.177
increases the output toward the reference.

27
00:01:40.322 --> 00:01:44.034
The proportional term responds to the error
that exists right now.

28
00:01:44.034 --> 00:01:47.886
Its contribution is K p times e of t.

29
00:01:47.886 --> 00:01:51.522
If the error doubles, the proportional
correction doubles.

30
00:01:51.522 --> 00:01:58.434
Increasing K p usually makes the response more
forceful and can reduce rise time, but

31
00:01:58.434 --> 00:02:02.121
excessive gain can produce overshoot,
oscillation, or instability.

32
00:02:02.121 --> 00:02:06.445
Proportional action alone may also leave a
steady offset.

33
00:02:06.445 --> 00:02:13.358
To hold a load against friction, for example,
the actuator may require a nonzero command.

34
00:02:13.358 --> 00:02:19.481
If command is proportional to error, a
persistent error may be needed to produce that

35
00:02:19.481 --> 00:02:19.889
command.

36
00:02:19.889 --> 00:02:25.195
This is not a defect in the formula; it is a
consequence of the loop and plant.

37
00:02:25.195 --> 00:02:30.661
The important skill is to connect gain to
observed response rather than memorizing that

38
00:02:30.661 --> 00:02:32.223
larger is always better.

39
00:02:32.354 --> 00:02:35.505
The integral term responds to the history of
error.

40
00:02:35.505 --> 00:02:40.569
Its state grows while error has one sign and
shrinks when the sign reverses.

41
00:02:40.569 --> 00:02:46.230
A small error that persists can therefore
produce a large enough correction to eliminate

42
00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:48.656
the offset left by proportional control.

43
00:02:48.656 --> 00:02:53.120
This makes integral action powerful, but it
also introduces memory.

44
00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:59.334
If the actuator saturates at its maximum while
the error remains large, the integral state

45
00:02:59.334 --> 00:03:03.477
can continue accumulating even though the
plant cannot respond further.

46
00:03:03.477 --> 00:03:09.185
When the system finally approaches the target,
that stored integral action may drive it far

47
00:03:09.185 --> 00:03:10.326
past the reference.

48
00:03:10.326 --> 00:03:12.393
This is called integral windup.

49
00:03:12.393 --> 00:03:17.970
Practical controllers limit, pause, or
back-calculate the integral state during

50
00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:18.528
saturation.

51
00:03:18.528 --> 00:03:24.687
Integral action solves one problem while
creating a responsibility to manage its stored

52
00:03:24.687 --> 00:03:25.161
state.

53
00:03:25.298 --> 00:03:28.959
The derivative term reacts to how quickly the
error is changing.

54
00:03:28.959 --> 00:03:34.974
If the output is racing toward the target, the
error may still be positive but decreasing

55
00:03:34.974 --> 00:03:35.350
rapidly.

56
00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:41.153
A derivative contribution can oppose that
motion before the target is crossed, adding

57
00:03:41.153 --> 00:03:42.939
damping and reducing overshoot.

58
00:03:42.939 --> 00:03:48.912
The difficulty is that differentiation
amplifies rapid measurement changes, including

59
00:03:48.912 --> 00:03:50.107
sensor noise.

60
00:03:50.107 --> 00:03:56.535
Real implementations therefore use a filtered
derivative rather than an ideal mathematical

61
00:03:56.535 --> 00:03:57.071
derivative.

62
00:03:57.071 --> 00:04:01.904
Many controllers also differentiate the
measurement instead of the error, which avoids

63
00:04:01.904 --> 00:04:05.528
a large derivative kick when the reference
changes suddenly.

64
00:04:05.528 --> 00:04:08.232
Derivative action is not mandatory.

65
00:04:08.232 --> 00:04:13.686
A well-tuned PI controller is often
sufficient, especially when sensors are noisy

66
00:04:13.686 --> 00:04:16.867
or the plant already has adequate damping.

67
00:04:16.994 --> 00:04:21.944
A PID controller adds the three contributions
into one command.

68
00:04:21.944 --> 00:04:27.477
Proportional action handles present error,
integral action handles accumulated error, and

69
00:04:27.477 --> 00:04:29.992
derivative action responds to trend.

70
00:04:29.992 --> 00:04:34.724
These descriptions are useful, but the terms
interact through the same plant.

71
00:04:34.724 --> 00:04:40.324
Increasing K p changes the response that the
integral and derivative terms observe.

72
00:04:40.324 --> 00:04:45.260
Increasing K i may remove offset while
reducing stability margin.

73
00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:50.655
Increasing K d may add damping while
increasing noise sensitivity.

74
00:04:50.655 --> 00:04:57.184
The final command is also bounded by actuator
voltage, force, valve opening, or another

75
00:04:57.184 --> 00:04:58.117
physical limit.

76
00:04:58.117 --> 00:05:04.231
A controller equation without limits, sample
time, sensor characteristics, and plant

77
00:05:04.231 --> 00:05:05.898
dynamics is incomplete.

78
00:05:05.898 --> 00:05:12.694
Good tuning treats the loop as one system
rather than adjusting three independent knobs

79
00:05:12.694 --> 00:05:13.665
in isolation.

80
00:05:13.826 --> 00:05:19.770
A step response turns vague words like fast or
stable into measurable criteria.

81
00:05:19.770 --> 00:05:24.018
Rise time describes how quickly output moves
toward the new target.

82
00:05:24.018 --> 00:05:27.717
Peak overshoot measures how far it exceeds the
target.

83
00:05:27.717 --> 00:05:32.819
Settling time measures how long it takes to
remain inside an acceptable band.

84
00:05:32.819 --> 00:05:37.487
Steady-state error is the remaining difference
after transients decay.

85
00:05:37.487 --> 00:05:39.426
These measures can conflict.

86
00:05:39.426 --> 00:05:44.209
A gain change that shortens rise time may
increase overshoot.

87
00:05:44.209 --> 00:05:48.839
Integral action that removes final error may
lengthen settling.

88
00:05:48.839 --> 00:05:55.268
Stability is the first requirement: a fast
response that grows without bound is not

89
00:05:55.268 --> 00:05:55.727
useful.

90
00:05:55.727 --> 00:06:00.676
Before tuning, define which measures matter
for the application and what constraints

91
00:06:00.676 --> 00:06:01.913
cannot be violated.

92
00:06:01.913 --> 00:06:08.307
A camera gimbal, temperature chamber, and
chemical flow controller should not share one

93
00:06:08.307 --> 00:06:09.783
generic tuning objective.

94
00:06:09.938 --> 00:06:14.594
A disciplined tuning process begins with
safety and requirements.

95
00:06:14.594 --> 00:06:19.849
Confirm sensor polarity, actuator limits, and
a safe test range.

96
00:06:19.849 --> 00:06:23.127
Start with integral and derivative action
disabled.

97
00:06:23.127 --> 00:06:29.041
Increase proportional gain until the system
responds clearly without unacceptable

98
00:06:29.041 --> 00:06:29.632
oscillation.

99
00:06:29.632 --> 00:06:35.519
Add integral action gradually to remove
persistent error, while watching overshoot and

100
00:06:35.519 --> 00:06:36.010
windup.

101
00:06:36.010 --> 00:06:41.675
Add derivative action only when extra damping
is needed and the measurement is clean enough

102
00:06:41.675 --> 00:06:42.808
to support it.

103
00:06:42.808 --> 00:06:49.518
Then test reference changes, disturbances,
load variation, saturation, and noise.

104
00:06:49.518 --> 00:06:55.760
This sequence is not a universal proof of
optimality, but it makes cause and effect

105
00:06:55.760 --> 00:06:56.176
observable.

106
00:06:56.176 --> 00:07:02.635
More formal model-based methods can calculate
gains, yet every real implementation still

107
00:07:02.635 --> 00:07:06.941
requires validation against unmodeled behavior
and physical limits.

108
00:07:07.082 --> 00:07:13.241
Most PID controllers run in software, so the
continuous equation becomes a sampled

109
00:07:13.241 --> 00:07:13.715
algorithm.

110
00:07:13.715 --> 00:07:19.998
The sample period must be short enough to
observe relevant plant dynamics but not so

111
00:07:19.998 --> 00:07:21.674
short that noise dominates.

112
00:07:21.674 --> 00:07:24.417
The integral becomes a running numerical sum.

113
00:07:24.417 --> 00:07:28.996
The derivative becomes a filtered difference
between samples.

114
00:07:28.996 --> 00:07:34.907
Output clamping enforces actuator limits, and
anti-windup keeps the integral state

115
00:07:34.907 --> 00:07:37.057
consistent with those limits.

116
00:07:37.057 --> 00:07:41.713
Systems that switch between manual and
automatic control also need bumpless transfer

117
00:07:41.713 --> 00:07:46.368
so the stored controller state does not create
a sudden command jump.

118
00:07:46.368 --> 00:07:54.303
Record the sample time and exact algorithm
with the gains; K p, K i, and K d have meaning

119
00:07:54.303 --> 00:07:57.644
only within a defined implementation and unit
convention.

120
00:07:57.770 --> 00:08:01.686
A reliable PID analysis answers six questions.

121
00:08:01.686 --> 00:08:07.477
What are the reference, measured output,
error, command, plant, and disturbances?

122
00:08:07.477 --> 00:08:13.702
What behavior does proportional, integral, and
derivative action contribute in this loop?

123
00:08:13.702 --> 00:08:16.686
Which performance measures define success?

124
00:08:16.686 --> 00:08:19.378
Where can the actuator or sensor saturate?

125
00:08:19.378 --> 00:08:24.250
How are sample time, filtering, and
anti-windup implemented?

126
00:08:24.250 --> 00:08:28.663
And has the design been tested across the real
operating range?

127
00:08:28.663 --> 00:08:31.776
The goal is not to use all three letters.

128
00:08:31.776 --> 00:08:37.086
The goal is a stable, understandable
controller that meets requirements with

129
00:08:37.086 --> 00:08:38.051
appropriate complexity.

130
00:08:38.051 --> 00:08:43.166
Sometimes that is P, often PI, and sometimes
full PID.

131
00:08:43.166 --> 00:08:48.426
If you can explain the loop and predict the
consequence of each change before turning a

132
00:08:48.426 --> 00:08:52.043
gain, you are tuning as an engineer rather
than searching blindly.
